---
title: >-
  Estimating Unique Users for Arbitrary Genre Combinations with a Bernoulli
  Mixture Model
draft: true
draft-mode: unlinked
date: "2026-09-23"
categories: ["reach-frequency", "statistics"]
---



A media planner asks: how many unique users watched K-Drama or Romance in the
past 90 days? The answer decides whether a proposed targeting segment can absorb
a campaign budget, and it must be produced for whatever genre combination the
planner selects, out of the $2^{16} - 1$ possible combinations of a 16-genre
catalog.

The setting for this post is a viewer panel: a sample of $n = 50{,}000$ viewers,
each described by $J = 16$ binary genre flags, where a viewer can be positive on
any subset of genres. The unique user (UU) count in the full population must be
estimated from this sample. With complete logs the question is answered exactly
by counting over the observed flag patterns, and no model is needed. With a
panel, rare combinations are observed for a handful of respondents or not at
all, and direct counts stop being usable.

UU factors as
$\text{UU}(S) = M \cdot P\!\left(\bigcup_{j \in S} X_j = 1\right)$, where $M$ is
the population size, a known constant. Everything below therefore works on the
probability scale. The deliverable is a function that maps an arbitrary genre
subset $S$ to the union probability without access to the raw data at query
time. This post builds that function by fitting a Bernoulli mixture model to the
panel and deriving the query layer in closed form from the fitted parameters.

## Synthetic data: sixteen genres, four latent factors

All results use synthetic data with a fully disclosed generating process. The
design goal is realistic co-occurrence: genre flags are correlated, and the
correlation has block structure.

Each viewer $i$ has a vector of four latent factors
$\mathbf{f}_i \sim N(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{I}_4)$. The genre flags are generated
independently given the factors:

$$
  P(X_{ij} = 1 \mid \mathbf{f}_i) = \sigma\!\left(\alpha_j + \boldsymbol{\lambda}_j^\top \mathbf{f}_i\right), \qquad \sigma(x) = \frac{1}{1 + e^{-x}}
$$

Factor 1 drives serious nonfiction (Crime Thriller, True Crime, Documentary).
Factor 2 drives romance (K-Drama, Romance, Reality Dating). Factor 3 drives
genre fiction (Anime, Sci-Fi, Fantasy, Horror). Factor 4 is total viewing volume
and loads weakly on every genre: heavy viewers watch more of everything, which
produces the global positive correlation observed in real viewing data.

