The generating model is a continuous factor model, not a finite mixture. The
estimation model introduced below is therefore misspecified by construction,
which reproduces the realistic situation: no real dataset follows a Bernoulli
mixture.

Each $\alpha_j$ was tuned by simulation, nudged until the marginals felt right,
spreading the viewing rates from 12% to 60%. The training panel is
$n = 50{,}000$ viewers. The evaluation truth is the empirical union probability
on an independent out-of-sample draw of 2,000,000 viewers from the same process.



## The independence approximation overstates the union

The simplest estimator treats the flags as independent. With marginal rates
$\hat{p}_j$ from the panel:

$$
  \hat{P}_{\text{indep}}\!\left(\bigcup_{j \in S} X_j = 1\right) = 1 - \prod_{j \in S} (1 - \hat{p}_j)
$$

Genre flags are not independent. Viewers who watch K-Drama disproportionately
also watch Romance, so the two audiences overlap far more than independence
implies. The product understates the overlap and therefore overstates the union.
The error is one-sided, and it compounds as more genres enter $S$.

The training panel gives $\hat{p}_{\text{K-Drama}} = 0.35$ and
$\hat{p}_{\text{Romance}} = 0.40$. The independence estimate of the union is
$1 - 0.65 \times 0.60 = 0.61$. The out-of-sample truth is about $0.55$: an
overestimate of roughly 11%. @tbl-indep shows the exact values. Scaled to a
population of 20 million, a planner would be told 12.2 million reachable users
when the true number is about 10.9 million.


