## A Bernoulli mixture as a density estimator

What the independence estimator gets wrong is co-occurrence; what it gets right
is closed-form evaluation. The Bernoulli mixture model with $K$ components keeps
the second and repairs the first:

$$
  P(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \pi_k \prod_{j=1}^{J} \theta_{jk}^{x_j} (1 - \theta_{jk})^{1 - x_j}, \qquad \mathbf{x} \in \{0, 1\}^J
$$

where $\pi_k$ is the weight of class $k$ and $\theta_{jk}$ is the probability
that a viewer in class $k$ has genre $j$ flagged. Within a class the flags are
independent; all dependence in the population arises from mixing over classes.
This is the model behind latent class analysis (LCA) in psychometrics, fitted by
EM; the [flexmix](https://cran.r-project.org/package=flexmix) implementation is
used below.

The model has $K(J + 1) - 1$ free parameters: $KJ$ class-conditional rates and
$K - 1$ weights. This sits between the independence model ($J = 16$ parameters)
and the saturated contingency table ($2^{16} - 1 = 65{,}535$). $K$ alone moves
the model between these two endpoints.

At $K = 1$ the mixture collapses to
$\prod_j \theta_j^{x_j} (1 - \theta_j)^{1 - x_j}$, whose MLE is
$\hat{\theta}_j = \hat{p}_j$. The independence estimator of the previous section
is the one-component special case. Increasing $K$ starts from the naive
estimator and adds co-occurrence structure.

The classes are not interpreted. In segmentation work an LCA fit is judged by
whether the classes are meaningful, and identifiability and label switching are
live concerns. Here the mixture is a density estimator for $P(\mathbf{x})$, the
classes are basis functions, and the only criterion is out-of-sample accuracy of
the UU queries. Two parameterizations that induce the same distribution give
identical UU answers, so label switching is irrelevant for this use.

```r
library(flexmix)

# X_train: n x J binary matrix; K chosen in the next section
fit <- flexmix(X_train ~ 1, k = K, model = FLXMCmvbinary())

pi_hat <- prior(fit) # length K
theta_hat <- parameters(fit) # J x K matrix of theta_jk
```

## Closed-form UU for arbitrary queries

Conditional on class $k$ the flags are independent, so any event that fixes a
subset of flags factorizes within a class. For disjoint index sets $A$ (flags
required positive) and $B$ (flags required negative):

$$
  P\!\left(\bigcap_{j \in A} X_j = 1 \;\cap\; \bigcap_{j \in B} X_j = 0\right) = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \pi_k \prod_{j \in A} \theta_{jk} \prod_{j \in B} (1 - \theta_{jk})
$$

The union query is the complement of the all-negative event. The union over $S$
fails exactly when every flag in $S$ is zero, so:

$$
  P\!\left(\bigcup_{j \in S} X_j = 1\right) = 1 - \sum_{k=1}^{K} \pi_k \prod_{j \in S} (1 - \theta_{jk})
$$

Evaluation costs $O(K \lvert S \rvert)$ multiplications and touches only the
fitted parameters, not the raw data. Intersections and negations ("watched Anime
but not Horror") are already in the factorized form above, and any Boolean
combination of flags reduces to sums of such terms, so one fitted model serves
every query type.

## Out-of-sample accuracy as a function of K







Each $K \in \{2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 16\}$ is fitted on the training panel with
three random EM restarts, keeping the best log-likelihood; $K = 1$ needs no
fitting, since its MLE is the vector of marginal rates. Every 2-way (120
queries), 3-way (560), and 4-way (1,820) union is evaluated against the
out-of-sample truth. The metric is the relative error
$\lvert \hat{u} - u \rvert / u$, averaged within each query size.


