@fig-err-vs-k has three features. At $K = 1$ the mean error is largest for 4-way
queries and smallest for 2-way, matching the mechanism from the independence
section: each additional genre compounds the ignored overlap. The error drops
steeply in $K$: most of the reduction is realized by $K = 4$, and from $K = 6$
the mean error for every query size is a fraction of a percent. Beyond that the
curve flattens, with no visible overfitting up to $K = 16$. At $n = 50{,}000$,
even $K = 16$ has only 271 parameters, so the variance cost of additional
components stays negligible over this grid.

Every evaluated union contains a genre with a marginal rate of 0.20 or higher
(only Horror sits below 0.20, and every query has at least two genres), so its
union probability is at least $0.20$ and the relative sampling error of the
truth, $\sqrt{(1 - p) / (N p)}$, is at most
$\sqrt{0.8 / (2{,}000{,}000 \times 0.20)} \approx 0.14\%$; out-of-sample errors
below this resolution would be indistinguishable from noise in the truth. The
plateau sits above it, and 0.14% is the worst case, reached only by the smallest
unions: for typical 3-way and 4-way unions the truth noise is several times
smaller. The remaining error is real model error, the bias floor of the
misspecified mixture, not noise in the truth.

$K = 8$ is used below; larger $K$ adds no measurable improvement here. @tbl-nway
breaks the $K = 8$ error down by query size, including tail behavior across all
2,500 queries.


